WEBVTT
Kind: captions

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:17.200 position:63%
The painting you are about to see is without a&nbsp;
doubt one of the strangest we have ever seen on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:23.520 position:63%
this channel. In this painting, there are two&nbsp;
creatures sitting on a rock. The first one is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:30.080 position:63%
enormous. He has the torso of a muscular man and&nbsp;
the legs of a goat, black and covered in fur.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.080 --> 00:00:35.280 position:63%
He covers his ears with both hands, throws his&nbsp;
head back, and shows all his teeth in a scream&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:42.640 position:63%
of pure agony. The second one is a child, a pale&nbsp;
red-haired child with two small horns poking out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:48.960 position:63%
of his forehead, holding a golden flute to his&nbsp;
mouth. And the curious thing is that while he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:56.640 position:63%
plays, he does not look at his companion. Instead,&nbsp;
he stares directly at us. Dissonance is the name&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.640 --> 00:01:03.440 position:63%
the artist gave to this painting. It was painted&nbsp;
by Franz von Stuck in 1910 and today it is held&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:09.680 position:63%
at the museum Villa Stuck in Munich, the house&nbsp;
the artist himself designed and where he lived.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:15.680 position:63%
If you have been following this channel, you&nbsp;
already know. He is the painter of the Wild Hunt,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.680 --> 00:01:21.760 position:63%
the work in which we see the god Wan galloping&nbsp;
among the dead. an artist known for dark works&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.760 --> 00:01:28.400 position:63%
full of symbolism, mythology, and sensuality. And&nbsp;
a curious detail is that often designed the frames&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:34.320 position:63%
of his paintings himself as if they were part of&nbsp;
the work. And here the frame even announces the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:40.720 position:63%
title to us like the name of a piece of music. The&nbsp;
creatures we see are thorns, beings from ancient&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.720 --> 00:01:46.880 position:63%
mythology, half man and half goat. And they were&nbsp;
amongst favorite figures throughout his entire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.880 --> 00:01:53.200 position:63%
career. The most widely accepted reading of this&nbsp;
work is quite simple. According to the analyses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.200 --> 00:01:59.120 position:63%
that historians have made of it, dissonance should&nbsp;
be interpreted as a humorous painting. Under this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.120 --> 00:02:03.680 position:63%
reading, the young thorn would be trying to&nbsp;
play the flute, but he does it so badly that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.680 --> 00:02:09.600 position:63%
the noise he produces is making the older thorn&nbsp;
beside him suffer. That would explain the title.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:15.280 position:63%
It invites us to see this image as a comic scene,&nbsp;
almost a caricature, which is curious coming from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.280 --> 00:02:21.120 position:63%
a painter who was usually much darker. To be&nbsp;
honest with all of you, this is a reasonable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:26.640 position:63%
explanation. But the longer we spend in front of&nbsp;
this painting, the more details appear that do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:32.720 position:63%
not quite fit with what at first would seem to be&nbsp;
a simple joke. Let us start with the older thorn.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.720 --> 00:02:39.120 position:63%
If this were a comic scene, we would expect to see&nbsp;
a gesture of annoyance or a grimace of irritation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:45.440 position:63%
But what we see is something completely different.&nbsp;
His body is completely tense. He presses his head&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.440 --> 00:02:51.040 position:63%
with both hands. His eyes are shut tight and&nbsp;
his mouth is open in a scream showing all his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.040 --> 00:02:56.720 position:63%
teeth. He does not look like someone annoyed by&nbsp;
an out of tune melody. He looks like someone who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.720 --> 00:03:02.800 position:63%
is truly suffering. And this is when we must look&nbsp;
at the child. The first thing that stands out is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.800 --> 00:03:08.080 position:63%
that he does not look like the other form. The&nbsp;
adult has the legs of a goat, while the child,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.080 --> 00:03:13.200 position:63%
on the other hand, has completely human legs&nbsp;
and feet. The only thing that gives him away&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:19.440 position:63%
are those two small horns on his forehead. It is&nbsp;
as if he were a hybrid between the two natures.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.440 --> 00:03:25.120 position:63%
And then there is his gaze. He does not look at&nbsp;
the thorn suffering beside him, nor at the flute&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.120 --> 00:03:31.760 position:63%
he is holding. Instead, he looks directly at us&nbsp;
with wide open eyes and no expression at all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.760 --> 00:03:38.000 position:63%
as if he knew perfectly well what he was causing.&nbsp;
To understand the hidden symbolism of this flute,&nbsp;&nbsp;

